00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.360
I want to start off with talking to you about&nbsp;
three things that keep me up at night, right? 
&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.800
Three things: the first, and it may be, you&nbsp;
know, very common for you too, is climate change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:16.160
Climate change absolutely keeps me up at night.
The second thing that keeps me up at night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:25.600
is that people may have no idea that an artificial&nbsp;
intelligence is making a decision that directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:32.720
impacts their lives - what percentage interest&nbsp;
rate you get on your loan, whether you get that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:38.880
job that you applied for, whether your kid gets&nbsp;
into that college that they really want to go to.&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.000
Today AI is making decisions&nbsp;
that directly impact you.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:54.160
The third thing that keeps me up at night&nbsp;
is: even when people know that an AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:01.198
is making a decision about them, they may assume&nbsp;
that because it's not a fallible human with bias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:08.720
that somehow the AI is going to make a decision&nbsp;
that's morally or ethically squeaky clean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.120
and that could not be farther from the truth. 
&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:18.720
So, if you think about organizations&nbsp;
and what happens over 80% of the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.120
proof of concepts associated with artificial&nbsp;
intelligence actually gets stalled in testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:32.400
and more often than not it is because people&nbsp;
do not trust the results from that AI model.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.960
So, we're going to talk a lot about trust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:41.360
and when thinking about trust (I’m going to&nbsp;
switch colors here) there's actually five pillars.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.480
OK, when you're thinking about what does it&nbsp;
take to earn trust in an artificial intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:52.800
that's being made by your organization or being&nbsp;
procured by your organization: five pillars. 
&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.400
The first thing to be thinking about&nbsp;
is fairness. How can you ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:06.800
that the AI model is fair towards everybody in&nbsp;
particular historically underrepresented groups. 
&nbsp;

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:16.480
OK, the second is explainable is your AI model&nbsp;
explainable such that you'd be able to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:24.000
somebody, an end user, what data sets were being&nbsp;
used in order to curate that model, what methods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.440
what expertise was the data lineage in provenance&nbsp;
associated with, how that model was trained. 
&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:40.560
The third: robustness. Can you assure end users&nbsp;
that nobody can hack such an AI model such that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.880
a person could disadvantage&nbsp;
willfully other people and or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.080
make the results of that model benefit&nbsp;
one particular person over another?
&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:03:00.960
The fourth is transparency. Are you telling&nbsp;
people, right off the bat, that the AI model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.000
is indeed being used to make&nbsp;
that decision and are you giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.120
people access to a fact sheet or metadata so&nbsp;
that they can learn more about that model? 
	
&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.880
And the fifth one is: are you&nbsp;
assuring people's data privacy?&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:22.800
So, those are the five pillars. OK, now&nbsp;
IBM has come up with three principles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:30.320
when thinking about AI in an organization.
	
 
The first being that the purpose of artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:36.640
intelligence is really meant to be to augment&nbsp;
human intelligence not to replace it.
&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:46.160
The second is that data and the insights from&nbsp;
those data belong to the creator alone
	
&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:53.600
OK, and the third is that AI systems, and I would&nbsp;
opine the entire AI life cycle, really should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.240
transparent and explainable, right? 
 
So, so, those are the five pillars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:03.280
Now, the next thing I want you to remember as&nbsp;
you're thinking about this space of earning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:09.440
trust and artificial intelligence is that this is&nbsp;
not a technological challenge. It can't be solved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:17.360
with just throwing tools and tech over some kind&nbsp;
of fence. This is a socio-technological challenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:23.040
"Social" meaning people, people, people.&nbsp;
Socio-technological challenges because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:29.200
it's a socio-technological challenge it&nbsp;
must be addressed holistically, okay? 
&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.720
"Holistically" meaning there's three major things&nbsp;
that you should think about. I mentioned people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:42.640
people the culture of your organization, right?&nbsp;
Thinking about the diversity of your teams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:47.440
you know, your data science team. Who is curating&nbsp;
that data to train that model? How many women are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:52.080
on that team? How many minorities are on that&nbsp;
team, right? Think about diversity. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:58.320
know if you've ever heard of the the "wisdom of&nbsp;
crowds". That's actually a proven mathematical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:05.520
theory: the more diverse your group of people,&nbsp;
the less chance for error, and that is absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:13.040
true in the realm of artificial intelligence. 
 
The second thing is process or governance, right?&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:16.640
What is it that use your organization&nbsp;
what are you going to promise your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:24.320
both your employees as well as the market with&nbsp;
respect to what standards you're going to stand by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:29.760
for your AI model in terms of things like fairness&nbsp;
and explainability accountability, etc., right?
&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:40.320
And the third area is tooling, right? What are&nbsp;
the tools, AI engineering methods, frameworks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:47.120
that you can use in order to ensure these&nbsp;
things, ensure those five pillars, and we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

